===== /candidate/NOTES.md =====
     1	# Verification scope
     2	
     3	`spec.k` proves the implementation at the semantic transition boundary:
     4	
     5	- `odd-step` symbolically proves, for every positive odd loop value greater
     6	  than one and every accumulated value sequence, that the program appends that
     7	  value and changes `n` to `3*n+1`.
     8	- `even-step` symbolically proves, for every positive non-odd loop value greater
     9	  than one, that the accumulator is unchanged and `n` changes according to the
    10	  supplied semantics' Python floor division.
    11	- `exit-step` symbolically proves, for every accumulated sequence at `n == 1`,
    12	  that the program appends `1` and returns `sortVS` of the complete sequence.
    13	- Four end-to-end claims prove complete executions for `1`, `5`, `6`, and `7`.
    14	  The LLVM assertion harness additionally executes the 111-step trace from
    15	  `27`.
    16	
    17	Together, the three symbolic claims are a partial-correctness proof for every
    18	finite Collatz trace: induction on the number of loop transitions composes
    19	`odd-step`/`even-step` with `exit-step`. The supplied semantics models
    20	`sorted` by its documented trusted `sortVS` primitive in symbolic proofs and
    21	by insertion sort in the LLVM runtime.
    22	
    23	A universal termination theorem for every positive integer is intentionally
    24	not claimed. Such a theorem would settle the Collatz conjecture, which remains
    25	open; K reachability proves the function's result on executions that reach
    26	`1`.
